It's not a question of the language (although I've heard nothing
that would make me think it is better than C++ or Java).  As far
as I know, however, it is only available for Windows and Linux
on PC's.  The platform I play most often on is a Sparc.  More
generally, I think that portability should be an issue.

> > But Java is pretty much ubiquious, and it's portability
> > model seems eminently suited for something like Nethack.

> As much as there's been dismissal of a Java rewrite, it's the
> most obvious to me.  Moving platform to platform now means
> different inaries with all sorts of potential problems with
> save files, source code branches, etc.  Doing an engine from
> scratch in Java would resolve that set of issues.  Learning
> Java in on my list of nice to have items like learning
> Japanese - Not sure I'll ever see the motivation to do it.

There are some aspects of Java that I seriously don't like, but
it's not that bad, at least for smaller projects.  And its
portability model is a very strong argument for it, in this
case.

> > About the only question I'd have with regards to Java is the
> > user interface: I sort of like the ASCII in an xterm look
> > and feel, and I'm not sure how to get that with Java (short
> > of implementing my own text window with Swing---which may
> > not be that difficult).

> Would a Rogue-like-hack-like-nethack-like-TNG stick with the
> old map oriented, ASCII oriented user interface?  I would want
> that.

Me too!  That's why I raised the question.

It's obviously doable: at one end, JLabel has some sort of
support for HTML, I think so you could dump the entire level in
HTML, or you could use a simple grid of 24x80 JLabel, each with
a single character (not sure that that wouldn't be the best
solution, come to think of it); at the other, the 2D graphic
components have support for drawing characters, so you could
derive directly from JComponent, and do all of the artwork your
self.  I suspect, however, that some of the Text components
might make things a lot easier (although a first, very quick
glance, suggests that they all take over the keyboard when they
have focus, which isn't what we want).

> > > and I guess Python also

> I thought the intent of Python was projects smaller than this?

I don't know too much about intent.  I think the intent of AWK
was mainly one or two line programs, but I often use it for a
couple of hundred lines of code.  And I've seen some fairly big
projects in Python.  (But probably still a lot smaller than
Nethack.)

> > > (at least C++ would be the only one of those languages I'd
> > > want to work with for this project).  Regarding C++, it's
> > > my opinion that this can make things much easier than
> > > plain C.

> C++ does seem the most natural choice even though it does call
> for recompiling across platforms, code branches per port, etc.

That's the part which is bothering me.  As long as you're
targetting a small, bound set of compilers and systems, it's
quite managable, but I'd worry about it in this context.

Effective use of C++ also supposes some degree of discipline at
the project level.  I'm not sure how to organize this in this
context, with (probably) no personal meetings, etc.

